Description: hwdec/dmabuf_interop_gl: cope with drivers that cannot import planes as normalised textures
 Seen on Arm's proprietary Mali GL driver (libmali g24p0 / g29p1) on RK3588:
  - DRM_FORMAT_R16 (the luma plane of P010) imports as GL_R16UI, an integer
    texture, which a float sampler reads as 0: 10-bit video drew near-black.
    A luma plane imported as a one-channel integer format of its own depth
    (R16UI, R8UI) is re-wrapped as an integer texture (NEAREST filters), and
    the renderers sample it as one; no copy. Other planes imported as integer
    textures are copied into a normalised texture of the same texel size with
    glCopyImageSubData, because they go through the chroma scaler.
  - DRM_FORMAT_GR88 (the chroma plane of NV12) is refused with EGL_BAD_MATCH.
    It is imported as R16 (the same bytes) and copied into an RG8 texture.
    GR88 only: the raw copy would swap RG88's channels.
  - vo_gpu: such a plane is only known on the first map, after vo_gpu chose
    voluntary dumb mode (e.g. --profile=fast), which would sample it
    unfiltered at output size. vo_gpu now sets use_integer_conversion when a
    mapped hwdec plane is an integer texture and re-checks its features before
    the next frame (at most one frame renders unfiltered).
 Decided once per plane on the first map. Drivers that import these formats as
 normalised textures (Mesa) take the old path after one extra query.
 glCopyImageSubData is used only where GL 4.3 / GLES 3.2 or the ARB/EXT/OES
 copy_image extension provides it.
 .
 Known limit: gpu-next with --scaler-resizes-only=no and a crop/pan/zoom can
 route the integer luma plane to a linear-filtered scaler; libplacebo then logs
 "Failed dispatching scaler.. disabling" and falls back to a simpler scaler.
 .
 Written with Claude (Anthropic): the LLM drafted the code after measuring the
 driver's behaviour with stand-alone EGL probes and revised it after two
 rounds of AI review; the maintainer tested it on an Orange Pi 5B (10-bit
 HDR10 4K60 zero-copy at about 2 % dropped frames; pictures within one 8-bit
 step of copy-back decoding, no bias; 8-bit identical).
